    {
      "source": "hn",
      "title": "Ask HN: Do you want a router that has DD-WRT pre-installed?",
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50039291",
      "author": "Ixander",
      "external_id": "50039291",
      "summary": null,
      "why_read": null,
      "authors": [],
      "category": null,
      "published": "2026-10-11T02:37:33Z",
      "score": 109,
      "comments": 110,
      "hn_url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50039291",
      "snippet": "I sometimes run across comments where someone says they want an open-source router. I wonder if this is a viable idea for someone to start as a small company. What makes me hesitant is that, first, it looks like this already exists in several wireless and non-wireless routers (like the GL.iNet Flint or OpenWrt One), and second, I assume hackers would just make their own.<p>I have been flashing DD-WRT to my routers for many years. My current setup is an Archer C9 that connects via cable to an Archer C8, which then wirelessly bridges to an R7000. All of them run DD-WRT. If I had &quot;fun money,&quot; I would buy a low-power SFF PC with dual NICs, run OPNsense or OpenWrt, and use the wireless routers strictly as access points. Since I don&#x27;t have that much to spend, I look for older DD-WRT friendly routers, usually under $30, on Craigslist or Facebook Marketplace, and flash them myself",
      "links": []
    },
